A1. Where you find the business man when bad weather interferes with his golf. ~ OFFICE
A6. Insignia of the Knockers' Club. ~ HAMMER
A11. A cub reporter's dream. ~ BEAT
A12. The contender with Adam for first place. ~ APE
A14. All too blindly does the unsuspecting groom assume it. ~ YOKE
A15. Incomplete is the lass without one. ~ LAD
A16. Balances; or ties. ~ EVENS
A18. The snorers' bench. ~ PEW
A19. Otherwise. ~ OR
A20. What the parson does at the end of the ceremony. ~ BLESSES
A22. A direction (abr.). ~ SE
A23. The stenographer's delight, but not her charm. ~ GUM
A24. A cunning little sprite. ~ ELF
A26. Dad feels like one when the Christmas bills come in. ~ GOAT
A28. Residue; adds to the fun of tending the furnace. ~ ASH
A30. Notable periods (not the ends of sentences, Dora). ~ ERAS
A32. Other people have them but not you and I. ~ VICES
A33. His idea of her before he marries her. ~ IDEAL
A34. A town in England; also something that should be taken regularly. ~ BATH
A35. A Geo. M. Cohan chorus girl has plenty. ~ PEP
A37. The old-fashioned girl used them as bait. ~ PIES
A39. To place or put in position. ~ SET
A41. Try and catch this—found in water and most crossword puzzles. ~ EEL
A42. A preposition. ~ OF
A44. The farmer's alarm clock. ~ ROOSTER
A47. Accomplish. ~ DO
A48. Adjective applied to the maid who too long enjoys single blessedness. ~ OLD
A50. Peacocks and women do it. And not a few men, 'tis said. ~ PREEN
A51. In favor of. (Now, isn't that easy?) ~ FOR
A52. The more deadly of the equine species. ~ MARE
A54. A suffix. ~ ESS
A55. The he-flapper takes tremendous pains with it when combing his hair. ~ PART
A56. The alpha and omega of women's thoughts (pl.). ~ STYLES
A57. Give a woman one, studded with diamonds, and she'll endure any opera (pl.). ~ TIARAS

D1. A geometrical figure longer than it is broad. ~ OBLONG
D2. The henpeck's feeling toward his wife. ~ FEAR
D3. This is what some say crossword puzzling is. ~ FAD
D4. A neuter pronoun. ~ IT
D5. Something on the roof. ~ EAVE
D6. The harem of the gent in horizontal 44. ~ HENS
D7. Mine. ~ MY
D8. A cleaning utensil. ~ MOP
D9. Pieces out. ~ EKES
D10. What the widower usually does. ~ REWEDS
D13. Fleas and saxophones. ~ PESTS
D16. A stately shade tree. ~ ELM
D17. Observe. ~ SEE
D20. What you'd like to call some surgeons. ~ BUTCHER
D21. You could call Rip Van Winkle one. ~ SLEEPER
D23. By these—you know them a long way off. ~ GAITS
D25. One of the weaker sex. (Warning: This is rough slang.) ~ FRAIL
D27. Eggs (Latin). ~ OVA
D28. What Cleopatra used to help her shuffle off. ~ ASP
D29. A good place to carry it if you're sure-footed. ~ HIP
D31. A beverage no longer in good standing. ~ ALE
D34. Once women's most deadly weapons, now being rapidly displaced by vacuum cleaners. ~ BROOMS
D36. Relaxes. ~ EASES
D38. Flappers go in for these—and out with them. ~ SPORTS
D40. Hers is shingle-bobbed now. ~ TOP
D41. What the poet writes when he means "even." ~ EEN
D43. What stale beer is—and some jokes (?). ~ FLAT
D45. Natural metal (pl.). ~ ORES
D46. Going through fire and water was one in a more romantic age. ~ TEST
D47. The dumb girl they up and wrote a song about. ~ DORA
D49. How I am; vertical 31 could relieve me. ~ DRY
D51. The adjective that ought to replace "near" in "near beer." ~ FAR
D53. A railroad on stilts (abr.). ~ EL
D55. He comes in handy for paying bills. ~ PA


